text:
&gt; the president&#x27;s name will start with a letter in the first half of the alphabet<p>Sorry, this triggered my fact-checkitis. I opened Wikipedia&#x27;s list of presidents of the US and ran this in the web developer console:<p><pre><code>    Array.from(document.querySelectorAll(
        &#x27;#mwgA tr &gt; :nth-child(3) span[data-sort-value]&#x27;
    ))
    .map(x =&gt; x.getAttribute(&#x27;data-sort-value&#x27;)[0])
    .reduce(({total, firstHalf}, c) =&gt; ({
        total: total+1, 
        firstHalf: c &lt; &#x27;N&#x27; ? firstHalf+1 : firstHalf
    }), {total: 0, firstHalf: 0});
</code></pre>
and it responded<p><pre><code>    Object { total: 47, firstHalf: 31 }
</code></pre>
Then I used Agresti and Coull&#x27;s method for a 95 % confidence interval around this (add two successes and two failures, compute Wald interval as usual). That gives us<p><pre><code>    65 % ± 13 %
</code></pre>
In other words, the 95 % confidence interval of the proportion lies in the span from 52 % to 78 %. That <i>is</i> significantly different from the null hypothesis.<p>I didn&#x27;t expect it, but we might actually have reason to believe US presidents more often than not do have names starting with letters from the first half of the alphabet.<p>Edit: But I just realised English letter frequencies have 54 % of the probability mass in the first half of the alphabet, so that&#x27;s probably a better null hypothesis than the even split. It&#x27;s also compatible with the actual presidential statistics.
